WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.880
NATO.

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:02.360
You’ve probably heard of it.

00:00:02.360 --> 00:00:06.040
It stands for the North Atlantic Treaty Organization.

00:00:06.040 --> 00:00:07.120
And guess what?

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:08.740
It just turned 70!

00:00:08.740 --> 00:00:10.060
But what does it do?

00:00:10.060 --> 00:00:11.500
And do we still need it?

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:12.720
Let’s take a look.

00:00:15.140 --> 00:00:22.180
In the aftermath of World War II, North American and Western European countries banded together against Soviet aggression.

00:00:22.180 --> 00:00:27.880
They formed NATO, a political and military
alliance “founded on the principles of democracy,

00:00:27.880 --> 00:00:30.260
individual liberty and the rule of law.”

00:00:30.260 --> 00:00:34.720
NATO’s goal is to promote stability and
trust among Western democracies.

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:37.500
Its central idea is collective defense.

00:00:37.520 --> 00:00:43.040
This means an armed attack against one NATO
country is considered an attack against them all.

00:00:43.040 --> 00:00:46.500
But after the Cold War, NATO’s heavy focus
on Russia changed.

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:50.680
The group welcomed in new members, many from
the former Soviet bloc.

00:00:50.680 --> 00:00:56.280
It expanded its global role: launching peacekeeping
missions in places like the former Yugoslavia,

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:58.020
Afghanistan, and Libya.

00:00:58.020 --> 00:01:02.860
Today, NATO faces a new set of political and
organizational challenges.

00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:06.520
At the top of the list are rising tensions
between Russia and the West.

00:01:06.520 --> 00:01:09.600
After the fall of the Soviet Union, these
tensions had faded.

00:01:09.600 --> 00:01:13.860
But more recently, however, Russia modernized
its military, invaded its neighbors, and meddled

00:01:13.860 --> 00:01:16.680
in European and U.S. elections.

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:22.300
In response, NATO has ramped up military exercises
and opened command centers close to Russia’s

00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:25.980
borders in Estonia, Latvia, Lithuania, and
Poland.

00:01:25.980 --> 00:01:30.740
It's also launched a new cyber command to fight
growing threats from Russia and elsewhere.

00:01:30.740 --> 00:01:33.600
Yet NATO appears weakened by internal divisions.

00:01:33.600 --> 00:01:38.860
Rising nationalism and even a move toward
authoritarianism in member states like Turkey,

00:01:38.860 --> 00:01:43.000
Hungary, and Poland are undermining the founding
principles of the alliance.

00:01:43.000 --> 00:01:44.960
As is the United States.

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:48.640
NATO's most powerful member is now deeply
critical of the organization.

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:52.820
President Donald Trump is no fan of multilateral
organizations in general.

00:01:52.820 --> 00:01:58.520
And for NATO in particular, he has repeatedly blamed other members for not paying their fair share.

00:01:58.520 --> 00:02:00.140
On this, he has a point.

00:02:00.140 --> 00:02:04.260
Every NATO country has pledged to spend
2 percent of its GDP on defense.

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:08.400
Yet, out of its 29 members, only 9 countries do so.

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:11.920
On the other side of the argument are NATO
supporters.

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:15.840
They believe the alliance still plays a vital
role in the 21st century.

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:19.620
After 9/11, NATO countries
came to America’s defense.

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:24.880
They joined the war in Afghanistan, fighting
and dying alongside U.S. soldiers.

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:29.220
In fact, this was the only time in NATO’s
history that allies employed that collective

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:31.280
defense at the heart of the treaty.

00:02:31.280 --> 00:02:37.400
Looking to the future, NATO can be a potent force against terrorism, cybercrime, and Russian aggression.

00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:39.960
Sure, NATO may need updates.

00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:45.920
But its presence is vital to ensure that the
United States doesn’t have to face these threats alone.

